rows=[(442, 'rain-seen-again', 'Rain seen again', 'The speaker rediscovers childhood pleasure after dropping a piece of paper into rainwater. A modest sheet trembling in a shallow runnel preserves the changed way of seeing, without inventing children or a picturesque monsoon panorama.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 loose grey-blue watercolor with spare graphite. View downward at a shallow stream of leftover rain along the edge of an ordinary paved lane. One small blank irregularly folded scrap of white paper floats near the edge, slightly buckled and wet at one corner, its center still above water. It is a paper scrap, not an origami boat. A few small broken ripples lap around it, water moves diagonally and thins over uneven stone. Frame crops the lane with quiet pale space above, no road vista. Selective pencil detail around paper and one stone join; remaining water a light wash. No children, boats, lettering, flowers, dramatic reflections or floods. Small recovered delight, daylight after rain, no gloom or nostalgic sepia.'), (446, 'gold-among-ordinary-leaves', 'Gold among ordinary leaves', 'Amaltas links present wonder to a remembered epic landscape. A close, living branch lets that continuity remain in the poem; it avoids inventing Ram, a hermitage or a documentary site.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 botanical oil-pastel and gouache study of a cropped amaltas (golden shower) branch outdoors in warm late-spring daylight. Two naturally unequal pendant racemes of small five-petalled yellow flowers hang from an oblique woody branch, one cluster partly occluded by pinnate green foliage. Slim continuous stalks and a few unopened buds, flowers neither giant bells nor wisteria. The fuller raceme left of center, one shorter loose raceme near the cropped left edge; branch continues out of frame. Quiet warm grey-green ground across the right, suggestion of foliage without a landscape. Matte yellow strokes brighter where sun reaches, shadowed petals ochre, no glowing chandelier, gold jewelry, halo, epic characters, palace or lettering. Celebrate beauty without perfectly spaced florets or equal detail everywhere.'), (448, 'mending-with-care', 'Mending with care', 'The poem imagines age as a garment to be washed gently and stitched with love. A small repair underway expresses care and acceptance without a literal aging body or an ornamental nirvana scene.', 'Original horizontal 3:2 intimate chalk-and-gouache textile study. A piece of plain faded indigo cotton lies loosely folded on a pale surface, with one modest square of slightly lighter cotton laid over a worn thin area. A line of small pale hand stitches holds two edges of the patch; another edge remains unsewn and lifts a little. One short loose thread curves from the last stitch onto the fabric, no needle or scissors. Cloth folds follow gravity and compression, patch conforms to the underlying fold rather than floating flat. Cropped cloth at lower left, generous undisturbed pale space upper right. Selective soft chalk marks for weave near repair only; no decorative embroidery, spectacular tears, artificial stains, thread lettering, person or props. Gentle practical care, ordinary window light, no dramatic before-and-after transformation.')]
items=[]
